# Real-time / WebSocket — framework-agnostic

## Architecture
- **Singleton per endpoint.** One module owns the connection.
- Three layers: Transport → Protocol → Bridge.
- Place in `packages/ws-client/` for shared, `apps/<n>/src/services/ws/` for app-only.

## Connection lifecycle
- Connect lazily on first subscriber, eagerly on mount for game UIs.
- Heartbeat every 15–30 s; 2 missed pongs = disconnected.
- Reconnect with exponential backoff (1, 2, 5, 10, capped at 30 s) plus jitter.
- Resume cleanly with sequence id / resume token; server replays missed frames.
- "Reconnecting" banner after N attempts; "offline" after the cap.

## Back-pressure
- High-rate streams (>10 Hz): aggregate frames, flush via `requestAnimationFrame`.
- Buffer threshold (~200 frames): drop oldest non-critical; never silently drop ordered.
- Watch outbound `bufferedAmount` — throttle if it grows.

## Consistency
- Sequence ids on every relevant frame. Drop or reorder out-of-order.
- Periodic snapshots for state that must converge (scoreboards, balances).
- On reconnect without resume support: invalidate dependent caches, refetch.

## Errors & defensive UI
- zod-validate every inbound frame. Bad frame logs + drops, never crashes connection.
- Render: `idle` / `connecting` / `live` / `reconnecting` / `offline` / `degraded`.
- `aria-live="polite"` on connection status region.

## Security
- `wss://` in production. Auth in URL/first-message, never cookies.
- Strip PII from client-side frame logs.

## Testing
- Unit: protocol layer with deterministic frames.
- Integration: in-memory WS fake driving the bridge synchronously.
- Cover: connect → first → disconnect → reconnect → backlog drain → malformed → buffer overflow.
